{"purpose":"Define the baseline communication capability every device must have, regardless of its audio capability or architecture. This is the requirement that makes walkie useful at all.","content":"## Requirement\n\nA device must be able to send a text message to one named device, or broadcast to every device on the tailnet, and read what it has received — from a terminal, with no graphical environment.\n\n## Behaviour\n\n- **Direct message**: addressed to a single named device.\n- **Broadcast**: delivered to every device currently registered with the coordinator.\n- **Identity**: every message carries a client-generated ULID, so that redelivery is idempotent and duplicates can be discarded by the receiver rather than prevented on the wire.\n- **Timestamps**: each message carries the sender's send time and the coordinator's receive time. Both are retained, because clock skew between devices is expected and neither value alone is trustworthy.\n- **Ordering**: messages are ordered per conversation, not globally. A total order across the whole tailnet is not required and must not be assumed.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n1. A message sent from device A to device B, both online, is displayed on B.\n2. A broadcast from A is displayed on every other online device.\n3. Redelivering the same message ULID to a receiver produces one displayed message, not two.\n4. All of the above works on a build compiled **without** the audio build tag, on linux/amd64 and linux/arm64.\n\n## Constraint inherited from the vision\n\nThis capability must never acquire a dependency on the audio stack. It is the floor below which no device is allowed to fall."}